Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  UpdatedType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 05  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 05  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Same Day Surgery  WACC 3 Frozen Section Pathologist  Judith A Ferry  M D  Frozen Section Resident  Tracy L  Davis  M D  Frozen Section Consultant s   None Consulting Pathologist s   None Assigned Resident Path  Abner Louissaint  M D   Ph D  Reports To  MICHELLE C SPECHT  M D             Addended Report             Reason for Addendum  1  Additional Immunohistochemistry Results CLINICAL DATA  57 year old woman with excisional biopsy for microcalcifications associated with intraductal carcinoma and DCIS on biopsy  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  LYMPH NODE  LEFT AXILLA  SENTINEL  456   EXCISION  THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY IN 1 OF 1 LYMPH NODE  SEE NOTE   NOTE  Keratin positive tumor cells are not identified on three  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  3 Cam5 2 immunostained levels  BREAST  LEFT   EXCISION  INVASIVE MAMMARY CARCINOMA WITH MIXED LOBULAR AND DUCTAL FEATURES  IN SITU CARCINOMA OF AMBIGUOUS TYPE  WITH COMEDONECROSIS AND CALCIFICATIONS  SEE TABLE AND NOTE  ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA WITH CALCIFICATIONS  HEALING BIOPSY SITE  NOTE  Both the in situ and invasive carcinoma show ambiguous features with respect to ductal or lobular subtype  The architectural pattern of the invasive carcinoma in this sample favors lobular carcinoma  however  an immunohistochemical stain for E cadherin shows moderate cytoplasmic positivity with focal membrane accentuation  The in situ carcinoma has a predominantly solid architectural pattern with rare microacini  cellular dishesion  and areas of comedonecrosis and calcification  E cadherin staining is variable within the in situ component  from negative staining to moderate positivity with membrane accentuation  In view of the ambiguous features of the in situ carcinoma in this specimen  it would be prudent to treat it 